# FIRST Global Simulator

FIRST Global Simulator is Team Vietnam's complete browser-based environment for running and reviewing **FGC 2026: Igniting Innovation** matches. It combines a controlled lobby, an authoritative Rust game server, physical field play, live scoring, and a post-match review surface in one workflow.

It is built for practice, driver development, demonstrations, and game-design review. It is not an official FIRST® or FIRST® Global scoring system or rulebook; use the official event materials for competition decisions.

## The complete loop

1. Sign in and open the [dashboard](/dashboard).
2. Create a FGC 2026 lobby, claim a station, choose the saved robot revision for that station, and ready up.
3. The host locks the roster and starts the match. The lobby supplies the exact pack, seed, robot revisions, station claims, visibility, and limits to the match service.
4. Drive the Starter Bot, intake WILDFIRE, score through the Suppression Unit, and use the physically driven grooved wheels to climb the BRACE.
5. Review the finalized score after the field is allowed to settle, then use the match overview and diagnostics for debriefing.

The [match-play guide](/docs/match-play) describes the room lifecycle. Use [controls and cameras](/docs/controls) before driving, and use [FGC 2026 rules](/docs/game-rules) for the pack's scoring model.

## Simulation contract

Every live match has one authoritative simulation. The browser sends bounded, ordered control input; it does not decide scores, ownership, match time, robot specs, or field geometry. The server runs at a fixed 60 Hz, publishes state at 20 Hz, records typed match events, and sends the final outcome to the match service.

The field pack is the source of truth for collision, scoring, ball release, and active targets. The client predicts ordinary chassis motion for responsiveness, then reconciles to authoritative snapshots when a ball, BRACE, scoring volume, or other unusual contact requires it.

This separation is deliberate: it keeps matches fair, makes reconnection safe, and gives reviewers a single state to trust.

